#pragma ISANN 0.1.20
# install-passenger.ian — a node that only CALLS other nodes.
#
# No engine, no model, no docker, no GPU. `infer --nodes` has isannd dial the
# peer directly, so nothing runs locally and there is nothing to serve. Hence no
# `requires:` block: a laptop on integrated graphics is a fine passenger.
#
# Two rendezvous verbs, and they are not interchangeable:
#
#     rv use    the default for QUERIES  - where this node looks for others
#     rv home   where this node REGISTERS - what a provider needs
#
# A passenger wants `use`. Setting `home` too is harmless but advertises a node
# that is not offering anything.

# Who owns this node. Asked rather than hard-coded: "me" is only the alias the
# bootstrap suggests, and a recipe that assumes it fails on every node where
# someone typed something else.
#
# `-default` is what makes the bracket real. The prompt printed "[me]" long
# before anything implemented it - Enter handed on an empty string and the next
# line died with "--account required". The runtime now draws the bracket from
# this same value, so what is shown and what is used cannot drift apart.
alias := func read -default me "account alias (the wallet you created): ";
auth unlock --account ${alias};

# Who is out there. Shown BEFORE the address is asked for, not after: this is
# the table the address is copied from, and having it last meant typing an
# address you had not been shown yet. It reads the indexer catalogue rather
# than the RV, so it works before anything is bookmarked.
rv list -remote;

# The rendezvous is how nodes find each other. Asked, not hard-coded: this
# address differs per network, and a wrong one here is silent - commands just
# find nobody. Copy the ADDR from the table above, or press Enter for the one
# this build ships with.
rv_url := func read -default https://110.44.52.98:9000 "rendezvous url (ADDR from the table above): ";
rv add --alias kr --url ${rv_url};
rv use --alias kr;

# Who is reachable through that rendezvous now that one is set. This is where
# the id to put after --nodes comes from.
list nodes;
